# Configuration for paypalmcp. Copy to .env and set what you need.
#
# A commented-out setting shows the DEFAULT it takes when left unset. A BLANK value means there is
# no literal default to show; the comment above it says what happens when it is left unset.
#
# WHERE THIS FILE GOES. It is looked for beside the binary, then at PAYPALMCP_ENV, then in the
# project directory, then at /etc/paypalmcp/.env. PAYPALMCP_ENV names the file itself and has to
# be set in the environment the MCP client starts the server with. `paypalmcp app:config` prints
# the file that was actually read. A variable already set - exported, or passed by the MCP client -
# wins over the same key in this file.
#
# No setting here is a credential: each account's REST app is in the account inventory.

# The state directory: cached PayPal tokens, each account's request count, and the months of
# transactions already read - payers' names and emails among them. Created 0700. An installed
# binary MUST set this absolutely: by default it follows the working directory, which an MCP client
# chooses, and each directory would then keep its own budget.
PAYPALMCP_STATE_PATH=

# The account inventory, which holds each account's REST app. accounts.toml.example is the
# template. Left blank, it is accounts.toml in the state directory.
PAYPALMCP_ACCOUNTS_PATH=

# Requests the server may send one PayPal account in any 24 hours, unless the account's entry in
# the inventory sets daily_budget.
#PAYPALMCP_DAILY_BUDGET=500

# The zone a bare date such as 2026-03-01 is read in, as the whole of that day. Times PayPal
# reports keep their own offset.
#APP_TIMEZONE=UTC

# Seconds a request to PayPal may take, and to wait for the connection.
#PAYPAL_TIMEOUT=30
#PAYPAL_CONNECT_TIMEOUT=5
